{"ts":"2026-09-24T01:20:32.284Z","level":"info","channel":"browser","message":"[ARTIFACT_CREATED] research: current_state_open_source_speech_recogni.json (10 KB) · validated","data":{"taskId":"tsk_mueuhplu8d2c47038a","agentId":"research_agent","data":{"id":"art_mueuhxy2dd62d5a976","kind":"research","name":"current_state_open_source_speech_recogni.json","path":"/app/runs/run_mueuhplufcc00f9738/research/current_state_open_source_speech_recogni.json","size":9793,"validated":true,"meta":{"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open-source_software","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaza_genocide_recognition","https://infoscience.epfl.ch/handle/20.500.14299/98082","https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/n16-1030","https://doi.org/10.1162/089120100561737","http://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13971","https://doi.org/10.1561/2200000083"],"engines":[{"engine":"wikipedia","status":"OK","found":4},{"engine":"openalex","status":"OK","found":5},{"engine":"hackernews","status":"OK","found":0}]}}}}
{"ts":"2026-09-24T01:20:32.285Z","level":"info","channel":"browser","message":"[TOOL_COMPLETED] Research live sources complete: Retrieved 7 live sources (wikipedia:4, openalex:5, hackernews:0) and extracted 16 citable facts.","data":{"taskId":"tsk_mueuhplu8d2c47038a","agentId":"research_agent","data":{"stepId":"tsk_mueuhplu8d2c47038a_s01","toolId":"research.search","summary":"Retrieved 7 live sources (wikipedia:4, openalex:5, hackernews:0) and extracted 16 citable facts.","output":{"query":"current state open source speech recognition evidence comparison","sourceCount":7,"factCount":16,"engines":[{"engine":"wikipedia","status":"OK","found":4},{"engine":"openalex","status":"OK","found":5},{"engine":"hackernews","status":"OK","found":0}],"handle":"research","sources":[{"engine":"wikipedia","title":"Open-source software","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open-source_software","snippet":"Open-source software (OSS) is computer software whose source code is publicly available, allowing users to use, study, modify, and distribute it — in contrast with proprietary (closed-source) software. These abilities are typically granted through open licenses or, more rarely, by dedication to the public domain. The development of open-source software can be based on open collaboration, a decentralized model of production. It is commonly defined according to the Open Source Definition of the Open Source Initiative, but the precise meaning of \"open source\" remains contentious.","publishedAt":"2026-09-21T17:01:52Z","kind":"encyclopedia"},{"engine":"wikipedia","title":"Gaza genocide recognition","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaza_genocide_recognition","snippet":"\nSince October 2023, the government of Israel has been accused of committing genocide against the Palestinian population of the Gaza Strip by several states across the world. The government of South Africa formally accused the country of genocide in December that year in a case before the International Court of Justice, with 14 countries joining the case by December the next year. Explicit state positions on the genocide include recognition, denial, or deferral to the ultimate result of the pending case. Acts of genocide recognition in particular include acknowledgement of genocide in the adoption of laws, parliamentary resolutions, and official speeches. Some countries have been described as silent regarding the matter.","publishedAt":"2026-09-05T21:06:44Z","kind":"encyclopedia"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"Connectionist Speech Recognition: A Hybrid Approach","url":"https://infoscience.epfl.ch/handle/20.500.14299/98082","snippet":"BACKGROUNDList of Tables 5.1 Comparison of the recognition error rates (I=insertions, S=substitutions, D=deletions) obtained with 1-state phonemic HMMs with discrete emission probabilities, Gaussian emission probabilities, and outputs of a contextual MLP. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .94 6.1 Phonetic classification rates at the frame level obtained by standard approaches.\"Full Gaussian\" refers to the case of one Gaussian with full covariance matrix per phoneme, \"MLE\" refers to the case of one discrete likelihood density per phoneme estimated by counting, and \"MAP\" refers to the case of one discrete posterior probability density estimated by counting. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .111 6.2 Phonetic classification rates at the frame level obtained from different MLPs, compared with MLE.\"MLPa b-cd\" stands for an MLP with blocs (width of context) of","publishedAt":"1993","score":1134,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"Neural Architectures for Named Entity Recognition","url":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/n16-1030","snippet":"Guillaume Lample, Miguel Ballesteros, Sandeep Subramanian, Kazuya Kawakami, Chris Dyer. Proceedings of the 2016 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies. 2016.","publishedAt":"2016","score":4507,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"Dialogue Act Modeling for Automatic Tagging and Recognition of Conversational Speech","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/089120100561737","snippet":"We describe a statistical approach for modeling dialogue acts in conversational speech, i.e., speech-act-like units such as STATEMENT, Question, BACKCHANNEL, Agreement, Disagreement, and Apology. Our model detects and predicts dialogue acts based on lexical, collocational, and prosodic cues, as well as on the discourse coherence of the dialogue act sequence. The dialogue model is based on treating the discourse structure of a conversation as a hidden Markov model and the individual dialogue acts as observations emanating from the model states. Constraints on the likely sequence of dialogue acts are modeled via a dialogue act n-gram. The statistical dialogue grammar is combined with word n-grams, decision trees, and neural networks modeling the idiosyncratic lexical and prosodic manifestations of each dialogue act. We develop a probabilistic integration of speech recognition with dialogue","publishedAt":"2000","score":1134,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"LLaMA: Open and Efficient Foundation Language Models","url":"http://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13971","snippet":"We introduce LLaMA, a collection of foundation language models ranging from 7B to 65B parameters. We train our models on trillions of tokens, and show that it is possible to train state-of-the-art models using publicly available datasets exclusively, without resorting to proprietary and inaccessible datasets. In particular, LLaMA-13B outperforms GPT-3 (175B) on most benchmarks, and LLaMA-65B is competitive with the best models, Chinchilla-70B and PaLM-540B. We release all our models to the research community.","publishedAt":"2023","score":3978,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"Advances and Open Problems in Federated Learning","url":"https://doi.org/10.1561/2200000083","snippet":"Federated learning (FL) is a machine learning setting where many clients (e.g., mobile devices or whole organizations) collaboratively train a model under the orchestration of a central server (e.g., service provider), while keeping the training data decentralized. FL embodies the principles of focused data collection and minimization, and can mitigate many of the systemic privacy risks and costs resulting from traditional, centralized machine learning and data science approaches. Motivated by the explosive growth in FL research, this monograph discusses recent advances and presents an extensive collection of open problems and challenges.","publishedAt":"2020","score":5510,"kind":"scholarly"}],"facts":["Open-source software (OSS) is computer software whose source code is publicly available, allowing users to use, study, modify, and distribute it — in contrast with proprietary (closed-source) software.","These abilities are typically granted through open licenses or, more rarely, by dedication to the public domain.","The development of open-source software can be based on open collaboration, a decentralized model of production.","Since October 2023, the government of Israel has been accused of committing genocide against the Palestinian population of the Gaza Strip by several states across the world.","The government of South Africa formally accused the country of genocide in December that year in a case before the International Court of Justice, with 14 countries joining the case by December the next year.","Explicit state positions on the genocide include recognition, denial, or deferral to the ultimate result of the pending case.","BACKGROUNDList of Tables 5.1 Comparison of the recognition error rates (I=insertions, S=substitutions, D=deletions) obtained with 1-state phonemic HMMs with discrete emission probabilities, Gaussian emission probabilities, and outputs of a contextual MLP.",".113 6.3 Phonetic classification rates at the frame level obtained from contextual MLPs, compared with standard likelihoods (MLE) and a posteriori probabilities (MAP).R represents the parametrization ratio, i.e., the number of parameters divided by the number of training patterns.","Guillaume Lample, Miguel Ballesteros, Sandeep Subramanian, Kazuya Kawakami, Chris Dyer.","Proceedings of the 2016 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies.","We describe a statistical approach for modeling dialogue acts in conversational speech, i.e., speech-act-like units such as STATEMENT, Question, BACKCHANNEL, Agreement, Disagreement, and Apology.","Our model detects and predicts dialogue acts based on lexical, collocational, and prosodic cues, as well as on the discourse coherence of the dialogue act sequence.","We introduce LLaMA, a collection of foundation language models ranging from 7B to 65B parameters.","We train our models on trillions of tokens, and show that it is possible to train state-of-the-art models using publicly available datasets exclusively, without resorting to proprietary and inaccessible datasets.","Federated learning (FL) is a machine learning setting where many clients (e.g., mobile devices or whole organizations) collaboratively train a model under the orchestration of a central server (e.g., service provider), while keeping the training data decentralized.","FL embodies the principles of focused data collection and minimization, and can mitigate many of the systemic privacy risks and costs resulting from traditional, centralized machine learning and data science approaches."]}}}}
