{"ts":"2026-09-24T01:24:59.503Z","level":"info","channel":"browser","message":"[ARTIFACT_CREATED] research: 45_black_holes.json (8 KB) · validated","data":{"taskId":"tsk_mueuni1kb884b42be5","agentId":"research_agent","data":{"id":"art_mueuno4t47cf93ec42","kind":"research","name":"45_black_holes.json","path":"/app/runs/run_mueuni1kd7bd414241/research/45_black_holes.json","size":8661,"validated":true,"meta":{"sources":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.116.061102","https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/50.6.667","https://doi.org/10.1086/312838","https://doi.org/10.1088/0264-9381/26/16/163001","https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ab0ec7","https://blogs.futura-sciences.com/e-luminet/2018/03/07/45-years-black-hole-imaging-1-early-work-1972-1988/","https://blogs.futura-sciences.com/e-luminet/2018/03/07/45-years-black-hole-imaging-1-early-work-1972-1988/","https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42188182"],"engines":[{"engine":"wikipedia","status":"ERROR","found":0,"error":"Unexpected token 'Y', \"You are ma\"... is not valid JSON"},{"engine":"openalex","status":"OK","found":5},{"engine":"hackernews","status":"OK","found":5}]}}}}
{"ts":"2026-09-24T01:24:59.504Z","level":"info","channel":"browser","message":"[TOOL_COMPLETED] Research the topic on live sources complete: Retrieved 8 live sources (openalex:5, hackernews:5) and extracted 10 citable facts.","data":{"taskId":"tsk_mueuni1kb884b42be5","agentId":"research_agent","data":{"stepId":"tsk_mueuni1kb884b42be5_s01","toolId":"research.search","summary":"Retrieved 8 live sources (openalex:5, hackernews:5) and extracted 10 citable facts.","output":{"query":"45 black holes","sourceCount":8,"factCount":10,"engines":[{"engine":"wikipedia","status":"ERROR","found":0,"error":"Unexpected token 'Y', \"You are ma\"... is not valid JSON"},{"engine":"openalex","status":"OK","found":5},{"engine":"hackernews","status":"OK","found":5}],"handle":"research","sources":[{"engine":"openalex","title":"Observation of Gravitational Waves from a Binary Black Hole Merger","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.116.061102","snippet":"On September 14, 2015 at 09:50:45 UTC the two detectors of the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory simultaneously observed a transient gravitational-wave signal. The signal sweeps upwards in frequency from 35 to 250 Hz with a peak gravitational-wave strain of 1.0×10(-21). It matches the waveform predicted by general relativity for the inspiral and merger of a pair of black holes and the ringdown of the resulting single black hole. The signal was observed with a matched-filter signal-to-noise ratio of 24 and a false alarm rate estimated to be less than 1 event per 203,000 years, equivalent to a significance greater than 5.1σ. The source lies at a luminosity distance of 410(-180)(+160) Mpc corresponding to a redshift z=0.09(-0.04)(+0.03). In the source frame, the initial black hole masses are 36(-4)(+5)M⊙ and 29(-4)(+4)M⊙, and the final black hole mass is 62(-4)(+4)M⊙, with","publishedAt":"2016","score":14545,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"Evidence for a Black Hole in the X-Ray Transient GRS 1009–45","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/50.6.667","snippet":"We report on the result of an X-ray observation of the X-ray Nova Velorum 1993 (GRS 1009–45), made with ASCA on 1993 November 10-11. The energy spectrum was extremely soft and accompanied by a hard tail, characteristics of the Galactic black-hole binaries in the soft state. It is well represented by a two-component model consisting of a multicolor disk model or a general relativistic accretion disk model, both describing emission from an optically thick accretion disk, and a power-law component with a photon index of ∼ 2.5. A spectral analysis of the soft component allows an estimation of the mass of the central object. For the optically-estimated distance (> 1 kpc) and inclination (> 37°), the mass is estimated to be $\\gt 3.1 M_\\odot$. These results strongly support that the compact object is a black hole.","publishedAt":"1998","score":338,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"A Fundamental Relation between Supermassive Black Holes and Their Host Galaxies","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/312838","snippet":"The masses of supermassive black holes correlate almost perfectly with the velocity dispersions of their host bulges, M bh ∝ σ α , where α = 4.8 ± 0.5. The relation is much tighter than the relation between M bh and bulge luminosity, with a scatter no larger than expected on the basis of measurement error alone. Black hole masses recently estimated by Magorrian et al. lie systematically above the M bh -σ relation defined by more accurate mass estimates, some by as much as 2 orders of magnitude. The tightness of the M bh -σ relation implies a strong link between black hole formation and the properties of the stellar bulge.","publishedAt":"2000","score":4479,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"Quasinormal modes of black holes and black branes","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0264-9381/26/16/163001","snippet":"Quasinormal modes are eigenmodes of dissipative systems. Perturbations of classical gravitational backgrounds involving black holes or branes naturally lead to quasinormal modes. The analysis and classification of the quasinormal spectra require solving non-Hermitian eigenvalue problems for the associated linear differential equations. Within the recently developed gauge-gravity duality, these modes serve as an important tool for determining the near-equilibrium properties of strongly coupled quantum field theories, in particular their transport coefficients, such as viscosity, conductivity and diffusion constants. In astrophysics, the detection of quasinormal modes in gravitational wave experiments would allow precise measurements of the mass and spin of black holes as well as new tests of general relativity. This review is meant as an introduction to the subject, with a focus on the re","publishedAt":"2009","score":2351,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"openalex","title":"First M87 Event Horizon Telescope Results. I. The Shadow of the Supermassive Black Hole","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ab0ec7","snippet":"Abstract When surrounded by a transparent emission region, black holes are expected to reveal a dark shadow caused by gravitational light bending and photon capture at the event horizon. To image and study this phenomenon, we have assembled the Event Horizon Telescope, a global very long baseline interferometry array observing at a wavelength of 1.3 mm. This allows us to reconstruct event-horizon-scale images of the supermassive black hole candidate in the center of the giant elliptical galaxy M87. We have resolved the central compact radio source as an asymmetric bright emission ring with a diameter of 42 ± 3 μ as, which is circular and encompasses a central depression in brightness with a flux ratio ≳10:1. The emission ring is recovered using different calibration and imaging schemes, with its diameter and width remaining stable over four different observations carried out in different","publishedAt":"2019","score":4300,"kind":"scholarly"},{"engine":"hackernews","title":"40 Years of Black Hole Imaging (1): Early Work 1972-1988","url":"https://blogs.futura-sciences.com/e-luminet/2018/03/07/45-years-black-hole-imaging-1-early-work-1972-1988/","snippet":"Community discussion with 3 points","publishedAt":"2023-10-18T10:47:04Z","score":3,"kind":"community"},{"engine":"hackernews","title":"40 Years of Black Hole Imaging","url":"https://blogs.futura-sciences.com/e-luminet/2018/03/07/45-years-black-hole-imaging-1-early-work-1972-1988/","snippet":"Community discussion with 2 points","publishedAt":"2022-03-05T23:30:03Z","score":2,"kind":"community"},{"engine":"hackernews","title":"Discussion","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42188182","snippet":"Community discussion with 0 points","publishedAt":"2024-11-19T21:18:28Z","kind":"community"}],"facts":["On September 14, 2015 at 09:50:45 UTC the two detectors of the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory simultaneously observed a transient gravitational-wave signal.","The signal sweeps upwards in frequency from 35 to 250 Hz with a peak gravitational-wave strain of 1.0×10(-21).","We report on the result of an X-ray observation of the X-ray Nova Velorum 1993 (GRS 1009–45), made with ASCA on 1993 November 10-11.","The energy spectrum was extremely soft and accompanied by a hard tail, characteristics of the Galactic black-hole binaries in the soft state.","The masses of supermassive black holes correlate almost perfectly with the velocity dispersions of their host bulges, M bh ∝ σ α , where α = 4.8 ± 0.5.","The relation is much tighter than the relation between M bh and bulge luminosity, with a scatter no larger than expected on the basis of measurement error alone.","Perturbations of classical gravitational backgrounds involving black holes or branes naturally lead to quasinormal modes.","The analysis and classification of the quasinormal spectra require solving non-Hermitian eigenvalue problems for the associated linear differential equations.","Abstract When surrounded by a transparent emission region, black holes are expected to reveal a dark shadow caused by gravitational light bending and photon capture at the event horizon.","To image and study this phenomenon, we have assembled the Event Horizon Telescope, a global very long baseline interferometry array observing at a wavelength of 1.3 mm."]}}}}
